# Long-lived infrastructure names for the yesod fleet.
# network-guy, 2026-09-10. Source: live audit of both hypervisors.
#
# CANONICAL DOMAIN: internal.yesod.work -- and it is the ONLY domain here.
#
# Verified 2026-09-10 against public DNS: every name under internal.yesod.work
# returns NXDOMAIN publicly, and yesod.work itself has no wildcard. So a name
# that is missing here FAILS CLOSED rather than resolving to something else.
# Do not add a wildcard to yesod.work -- that property is load-bearing.
#
# lan.planetbarr.com ALIASES WERE REMOVED 2026-09-10, and this is a safety fix
# rather than tidying. The factory mayor measured that zone and found a PUBLIC
# WILDCARD: every name under it, including names that do not exist, resolves to
# 103.168.172.37 -- a stranger's host. A client not using our resolvers would
# have silently pointed its NTP and NAS endpoints at that address and the
# contract would have VALIDATED. 'the fleet cannot resolve its NTP server' is a
# far safer failure than 'the fleet is quietly using a stranger's NTP server'.
# The local=/lan.planetbarr.com/ line is KEPT in the dnsmasq config on purpose,
# so that any stale reference gets NXDOMAIN from us instead of the wildcard.
#
# RULE: only hosts that are long-lived AND stably addressed belong here.
# Ephemeral guests (gate demons, test databases, runners) are DELIBERATELY
# ABSENT -- they live in the DHCP pool .20.20-.119, are reborn into whatever
# address the pool hands out, and once dnsmasq owns VLAN 20 DHCP they register
# their own names at lease time. Adding one by hand is the same mistake as
# giving it a router reservation (vlan-cutover.md 18).

# --- physical hosts and appliances
192.168.20.202  seykhl.internal.yesod.work seykhl.lan seykhl
192.168.20.10   sefer.internal.yesod.work sefer.lan sefer
192.168.0.100   sefer-trusted.internal.yesod.work sefer-trusted.lan sefer-trusted
192.168.0.123   nas.internal.yesod.work nas.lan nas synology
192.168.20.1    router.internal.yesod.work router.lan router gateway gw
192.168.0.1     router-trusted.internal.yesod.work router-trusted.lan router-trusted gw-trusted

# --- service endpoint names (ys-yes-81xu) -- the dg5 inventory declares endpoints BY NAME and forbids literal_ip_addresses, so each shared service needs a name, including this resolver
192.168.20.202  dns.internal.yesod.work dns.lan dns ns resolver
192.168.20.202  ntp.internal.yesod.work ntp.lan ntp
192.168.20.1    dhcp.internal.yesod.work dhcp.lan dhcp
# Explicit per-server names for the redundant pair, added 2026-09-10 when
# sefer became the second resolver and second time source.
# NOTE: the bare 'dns' and 'ntp' names above deliberately still resolve to
# SEYKHL ONLY (.20.202). The dg5 fleet inventory contract was validated
# against those, and a name that suddenly returns two addresses could change
# behaviour in something that hashes or compares the resolved value. Use the
# numbered names when you specifically want the second server.
192.168.20.202  dns1.internal.yesod.work dns1.lan dns1
192.168.20.10   dns2.internal.yesod.work dns2.lan dns2
192.168.20.202  ntp1.internal.yesod.work ntp1.lan ntp1
192.168.20.10   ntp2.internal.yesod.work ntp2.lan ntp2

# --- VLAN 20 services (reservation range .120-.254)
192.168.20.138  dertog.internal.yesod.work dertog.lan dertog
192.168.20.150  doltsvr.internal.yesod.work doltsvr.lan doltsvr
192.168.20.154  seykhl-actions-runner.internal.yesod.work seykhl-actions-runner.lan seykhl-actions-runner
192.168.20.155  yesod-postgres-server.internal.yesod.work yesod-postgres-server.lan yesod-postgres-server postgres pg
192.168.20.157  litellm-gateway.internal.yesod.work litellm-gateway.lan litellm-gateway litellm
192.168.20.163  obs-vultr.internal.yesod.work obs-vultr.lan obs-vultr
192.168.20.167  neo4j.internal.yesod.work neo4j.lan neo4j
192.168.20.172  yesod-semantic-graph.internal.yesod.work yesod-semantic-graph.lan yesod-semantic-graph

# --- trusted-LAN services (not yet migrated)
192.168.0.139   docuseal.internal.yesod.work docuseal.lan docuseal
192.168.0.145   n8n-server.internal.yesod.work n8n-server.lan n8n-server n8n
192.168.0.147   aicoe-social-runner.internal.yesod.work aicoe-social-runner.lan aicoe-social-runner
192.168.0.149   drawio.internal.yesod.work drawio.lan drawio
192.168.0.169   bukher.internal.yesod.work bukher.lan bukher
192.168.0.170   makor.internal.yesod.work makor.lan makor
192.168.0.171   makor-runner-docker-1.internal.yesod.work makor-runner-docker-1.lan makor-runner-docker-1

# 2026-09-18 yes-6kl4s.33: retained controller log-relay SERVICE address (owned by the live controller as a /32,
# see ops/yesod-birth/controller-service-addresses.py). Long-lived and stably addressed, so it belongs here.
# The yesod-runner-g1-dispatch aliases heal the 15 hand-installed fleet collectors (demons 189-197, runners 169-174)
# that were rendered against VM152's DHCP-lease name; drop those aliases once .4 re-images/rebirths bake the literal address.
192.168.20.192  log-relay.internal.yesod.work log-relay.lan log-relay yesod-runner-g1-dispatch.internal.yesod.work yesod-runner-g1-dispatch

# Braintrust data cleaning v1, seykhl VMs 700-703 (btdcv1-26f.1).
192.168.20.180  dataclean-postgres.internal.yesod.work dataclean-postgres
192.168.20.181  dataclean-minio.internal.yesod.work dataclean-minio
192.168.20.182  dataclean-dagster.internal.yesod.work dataclean-dagster
192.168.20.183  dataclean-api.internal.yesod.work dataclean-api
